detail.abstract:
Tax-preparation agents are difficult to evaluate because they are not merely language models answering legal questions. They must identify authoritative sources, recognise source status, map fact patterns to tax-treatment outcomes, ask for missing governing facts, and maintain professional boundaries under adversarial pressure. They may also produce structured artefacts for professional review; however, this initial benchmark evaluates the narrower worked-example surface of source grounding, outcome alignment, source-status recognition, missing-fact handling, and boundary safety.&nbsp;
<div>
 We introduce TaxBench-AU, an authority-grounded benchmark for Australian tax worked examples. TaxBench-AU uses public Australian Taxation Office materials — including rulings, determinations, interpretative decisions, guides, and worked-example solutions — to construct auditable labels. This reduces reliance on scarce tax-domain experts for every gold label while preserving an audit path to public regulatory sources.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 TaxBench-AU is deterministic-first rather than deterministic-only. Every evaluated system emits the same structured JSON output schema. Programmatic checks score run completion, schema validity, and source recall against explicit ATO identifiers. A narrow cross-provider LLM judge scores substantive outcome alignment against the cited ATO position, including source-status and missing-fact discipline where those behaviours are expressed in the answer. A separate cross-provider judge scores professional-boundary safety. The benchmark avoids broad free-text similarity scoring and uses no canonical-label vocabularies in the scoring logic.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 We compare Agent A, a Sonnet-based tool-using tax-preparation agent with retrieval, web search, domain-specific knowledge modules, memory, structured workpaper tools, and verification gates, against raw Sonnet and raw Haiku baselines invoked through the same schema. Results on the held-out 60-item test split are reported as per-task pass rates, guardrail-failure counts, and error-class distributions. Because confidence intervals overlap on multiple tasks, we frame TaxBench-AU as an error-localisation diagnostic of tax-agent behaviour rather than a validation of autonomous tax preparation, regulatory compliance, or accountant-grade workpaper production.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 TaxBench-AU is not a certification benchmark for tax agents; it is a reproducible diagnostic for measuring whether systems align with public ATO worked examples and where their failures occur.
</div>
